---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import BondingSim from '../../simulators/bonding/BondingSim.astro';
import { bondingQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/bonding';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why atoms bond' },
  { id: 'covalent', label: 'Covalent bonds' },
  { id: 'ionic', label: 'Ionic bonds' },
  { id: 'electronegativity', label: 'Electronegativity and polarity' },
  { id: 'shapes', label: 'Molecular shapes (VSEPR)' },
  { id: 'structures', label: 'Structure and properties' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Chapter 7: Chemical Bonding and Molecular Geometry. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-introduction' },
  { text: 'Pauling, L. (1960). The Nature of the Chemical Bond (3rd ed.). Cornell University Press.' },
  { text: 'Gillespie, R. J. (2008). Fifty years of the VSEPR model. Coordination Chemistry Reviews, 252(12–14), 1315–1327.' },
  { text: 'Haynes, W. M. (Ed.). CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics. CRC Press (melting and boiling points).' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="chemical-bonding"
  description="Interactive 3D chemical bonding explorer: compare covalent molecules, ionic lattices and giant structures, see lone pairs, partial charges, molecular shapes and how bonding explains properties."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={bondingQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Everything around you is held together by chemical bonds: atoms sharing electrons, giving them away, or pulling on
      them unevenly. Rotate 3D models of molecules and crystals, see where the electrons are, and discover why water is
      bent, why salt melts at 801 °C and why diamond is so hard.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <BondingSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>substance</strong>. <strong>Drag</strong> to rotate it and scroll or pinch to zoom (tap first on touch screens).</li>
      <li>Count the sticks between atoms: one stick for a single bond, two for a double bond, three for a triple bond.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>lone pairs</strong> (yellow lobes) and see how they affect the shape. Compare water, ammonia and methane.</li>
      <li>Read the <strong>δ+ and δ−</strong> labels and the electronegativity readout to see how evenly electrons are shared.</li>
      <li>Compare the simple molecules with <strong>sodium chloride</strong>, <strong>magnesium oxide</strong> and <strong>diamond</strong>, including their melting points. <strong>Click any atom</strong> for details.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why atoms bond</h2>
  <p>
    Atoms are most stable when their outer electron shell is full: usually eight electrons (an <strong>octet</strong>), or
    two for hydrogen. The noble gases already have full shells, which is why they barely react. Other atoms reach a full
    shell by <strong>sharing</strong> electrons (covalent bonding) or by <strong>transferring</strong> them (ionic bonding).
    Bonding lowers the energy of the atoms, so energy is released when bonds form.
  </p>

  <h2 id="covalent">Covalent bonds</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>covalent bond</strong> is a pair of electrons shared between two atoms, usually non-metals. Both nuclei
    attract the shared pair, holding the atoms together. Atoms can share one, two or three pairs, forming
    <strong>single</strong>, <strong>double</strong> or <strong>triple bonds</strong>; more shared pairs make shorter,
    stronger bonds. Outer electron pairs that are not shared are called <strong>lone pairs</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ionic">Ionic bonds</h2>
  <p>
    When a metal reacts with a non-metal, the metal atom usually <strong>transfers</strong> its outer electrons to the
    non-metal. The metal becomes a positive ion (such as Na⁺ or Mg²⁺) and the non-metal a negative ion (Cl⁻ or O²⁻). These
    oppositely charged ions attract strongly in every direction, building a <strong>giant ionic lattice</strong>. There
    are no separate molecules: the formula NaCl just gives the ratio of ions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="electronegativity">Electronegativity and polarity</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Electronegativity</strong> measures how strongly an atom attracts shared electrons. On Linus Pauling’s scale,
    fluorine is highest (3.98) and the alkali metals lowest. The difference between two atoms predicts the bond type:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Less than about 0.4:</strong> non-polar covalent (electrons shared evenly), as in H₂ or C–H bonds.</li>
    <li><strong>About 0.4 to 1.8:</strong> polar covalent: the more electronegative atom gets a partial negative charge (δ−), as in O–H.</li>
    <li><strong>More than about 1.8:</strong> ionic: the electron is effectively transferred, as in NaCl.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These cut-offs are a guide; bonding is really a spectrum. Whether a whole molecule is polar also depends on its
    shape: the polar bonds in CO₂ cancel because the molecule is straight.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shapes">Molecular shapes (VSEPR)</h2>
  <p>
    Electron pairs around a central atom repel each other and spread out as far as possible. This is
    <strong>valence shell electron pair repulsion</strong> (VSEPR) theory. Four pairs point to the corners of a
    tetrahedron (109.5°), as in methane. Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs, squeezing the bond angles:
    107° in ammonia (one lone pair) and 104.5° in water (two lone pairs). Two pairs or double bonds on opposite sides give a
    linear molecule, like CO₂.
  </p>

  <h2 id="structures">Structure and properties</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Example</th><th>Melting point</th><th>Conducts electricity?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Simple molecular</td><td>Methane, water</td><td>Low (weak forces between molecules)</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Giant ionic</td><td>Sodium chloride</td><td>High</td><td>Only when melted or dissolved</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Giant covalent</td><td>Diamond</td><td>Very high</td><td>No (graphite is an exception)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Ion</dt>
    <dd>An atom or group of atoms that has lost or gained electrons, giving it a charge.</dd>
    <dt>Lone pair</dt>
    <dd>A pair of outer-shell electrons not involved in bonding.</dd>
    <dt>Dipole</dt>
    <dd>A separation of charge, with a slightly positive end and a slightly negative end.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Boiling water breaks the O–H bonds.”</strong> It only overcomes the forces between molecules; the molecules stay intact.</li>
    <li><strong>“Salt is made of NaCl molecules.”</strong> It is a continuous lattice of ions.</li>
    <li><strong>“Ionic and covalent are completely separate.”</strong> Bonds range smoothly from equal sharing to complete transfer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Covalent bonds share electron pairs; ionic bonds transfer electrons to form oppositely charged ions.</li>
    <li>Electronegativity differences predict whether a bond is non-polar, polar or ionic.</li>
    <li>Repulsion between electron pairs, especially lone pairs, sets a molecule’s shape.</li>
    <li>Structure explains properties: small molecules melt easily; giant lattices and networks do not.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: atoms are drawn as spheres and bonds as sticks; real electrons form fuzzy clouds. Lone-pair lobes are shown approximately. Only a small piece of each giant structure is drawn.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
